            "reply_data": [
                {
                    "author": {
                        "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/45304579?u=59539f800b4ed5c30cbfa66108fb638966bffb14&v=4",
                        "login": "jamestansx",
                        "resourcePath": "/jamestansx",
                        "url": "https://github.com/jamestansx"
                    },
                    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
                    "body": "Thanks for the quick answer!\r\n\r\n> but always setting it to 1 seems like a good design because it enforces the biggest vertical space it can get.\r\n\r\nAgreed. But I prefer my statusline position to be static, I don't mind losing one column. I think it's very rare that one might set their cmdheight > 3 (right?)",
                    "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Thanks for the quick answer!</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">but always setting it to 1 seems like a good design because it enforces the biggest vertical space it can get.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Agreed. But I prefer my statusline position to be static, I don't mind losing one column. I think it's very rare that one might set their cmdheight &gt; 3 (right?)</p>",
                    "bodyText": "Thanks for the quick answer!\n\nbut always setting it to 1 seems like a good design because it enforces the biggest vertical space it can get.\n\nAgreed. But I prefer my statusline position to be static, I don't mind losing one column. I think it's very rare that one might set their cmdheight > 3 (right?)",
